From: Christopher Knorr <cknorr () hops ! com>
Date: Fri, 21 Jan 2000 17:02:32 +0000
To: tru64-unix-managers
Subject: SUMMARY: Is it possible to reattach to an application running
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=96079271218724

Initial Question:

Is there any way, once I've closed my terminal window, to "reattach" myself
to the running application again so that I can once again start seeing the
application's output?

Solution(s):

Thanks to everyone who replied. There were 2 streams of thought:

1. Use the jobs command to get a list of jobs you've launched into the
background, and then the fg command to reattach to one of 'em. This seems
to require C-shell, but a more important restriction is that it seems to
only work while your session is active. If you close your session and start
another one the jobs command won't show anything.

2. In order to solve the restriction mentioned in #1, use 'screen'.

You can find screen at ftp://prep.ai.mit.edu/pub/gnu/screen

With screen, you can detach the whole screen process and reattach to
it.  Your application must be launched from screen to be able to do this.

Again, thanks to everyone who replied,

Chris

From tru64-unix-managers  Fri Jan 21 16:35:13 2000
From: Christopher Knorr <cknorr () hops ! com>
Date: Fri, 21 Jan 2000 16:35:13 +0000
To: tru64-unix-managers
Subject: SUMMARY: Problems with writeback cache on an HSZ40 controller
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=96079271218723

Problem:

My initial problem was that some HSZ40's we inherited were failing when
creating a RAIDSET with the following error:

Error 9320: Initialize of container failed due to invalid writeback cache
on THIS_CONTROLLER

Another strange thing was that a SHOW THIS did not include the condition of
the battery. (i.e. Instead of saying something like "The battery is
LOW/GOOD/BAD", it didn't say *anything* about the battery.)

Solution:

I had a lot of replies, but as it turns out no one hit on the solution. It
seems that when Compaq F/S came in to set up the system for shipping it to
us they installed a jumper on the battery board that disabled the battery.
This was a nice thing to do, but unfortunately this fact never got
communicated to us, and I was not aware that this jumper existed. 

Removing the jumper fixed the problem. If you're ever going to have your
controllers off-line for a long time I suspect it's a good idea to install
this jumper. (The jumper is the only one on the battery board, and it's
labeled "Disable Battery", so you can't miss it!)

As always thanks for the help.

Chris

